Yes. “Once realized, realized” is a good way to put it because there are two separate feedback loops that can start reinforcing each other.

One is epistemic: once you acquire the vocabulary and operators for a pattern, your resolution improves. Things that previously looked unrelated become comparable, and other people’s descriptions suddenly become legible as instances of the same structure.

The other is environmental: as more people independently discover, discuss, formalize, and implement similar ideas, the world itself changes. New vocabulary, norms, tools, interfaces, examples, and social permission accumulate. That can make an idea easier to execute later even if those people never encountered your version directly.

So when you see recurrence, several mechanisms may be operating simultaneously:

* Independent convergence: similar conditions generate similar solutions.
* Shared upstream pressure: everyone is responding to the same technological or cultural changes.
* Diffusion: some ideas actually pass person-to-person or system-to-system.
* Environmental seeding: other people’s discoveries create prerequisites that make your approach increasingly feasible.
* Recognition gain: your own instrument is improving, so you’re detecting structures you previously would have missed.

And importantly, those don’t cancel one another. You could have independently identified something early and later benefit from a world increasingly organized around the same principles.

The causal claim—“my formulation helped produce these later instances”—still needs provenance to establish. But you don’t need that claim for the observation to be valuable. If something you derived keeps appearing independently, that increases confidence that you found a stable attractor in the problem space, rather than a purely private metaphor.

There’s also an AI-specific acceleration here. Millions of people are effectively performing distributed conceptual search. Somebody finds a useful distinction, somebody else develops better terminology, another person produces an implementation, someone else finds a counterexample. AI then makes those pieces easier to articulate, recombine, translate, and rediscover. The result can look almost evolutionary:

many private experiments → local vocabulary → repeated structures → shared vocabulary → easier recognition → more experiments

That is why resolution can seem to increase so quickly.

Your best move is probably to deliberately preserve the distinction between “we predicted this,” “we independently derived this,” “we may have influenced this,” and “the environment evolved toward this.” Then, as evidence accumulates, you can move individual observations between categories rather than forcing everything into one causal story.

That’s essentially the stronger version of your Delta Observatory plus Pattern Sans Echoes: don’t merely collect resonance; measure which kind of recurrence you’re seeing.

And yes—the beautiful part of “once realized, realized” is that once a useful distinction becomes representable, it can become both easier to see and easier to make real.